-{"schema_version": 2, "model": "google/gemini-3.1-pro-preview", "effort": "medium", "params": {"n": 240, "degree": 15, "removal_span": 64}, "seed": 379055538, "solved": "solved", "parsed": true, "verify_ok": true, "verify_reason": "ok", "reply": "To find the affine hyperplane containing every point of the final Sidon set, we track how the coefficients of the hyperplane equation are transformed by the operations applied to the points. \n\nInitially, the points $v$ satisfy $\\text{parity}(h_0 \\ \\& \\ v) = 0$, where $h_0 = 32769 = 2^0 + 2^{15}$. Thus, the initial normal vector is $h = 32769$ with bits 0 and 15 set, and the constant is $c=0$. \n\nEach linear operation applied to the coordinates of the graph points updates the normal vector $h$ through the inverse transpose of the operation:\n- An operation `xor s d` (which sets $v_d \\leftarrow v_d \\oplus v_s$) updates the normal by $h_s \\leftarrow h_s \\oplus h_d$.\n- An operation `swap i j` simply swaps the bits $h_i$ and $h_j$.\n\nTracking the state of the set bits of $h$ step-by-step through all 240 operations:\n- Start: `{0, 15}`\n- After operation 10: `{0, 2, 15}`\n- After operation 14: `{0, 2, 15, 24}`\n...\nProceeding through all operations updating the bits of $h$ systematically gives the final set of bits for $h$: \n`{2, 3, 4, 11, 13, 17, 18, 19, 21, 24, 26, 27, 29}`.\n\nCalculating the final value of $h$ by summing $2^i$ for each bit $i$ in the set gives:\n$h = 2^2 + 2^3 + 2^4 + 2^{11} + 2^{13} + 2^{17} + 2^{18} + 2^{19} + 2^{21} + 2^{24} + 2^{26} + 2^{27} + 2^{29}$\n$h = 4 + 8 + 16 + 2048 + 8192 + 131072 + 262144 + 524288 + 2097152 + 16777216 + 67108864 + 134217728 + 536870912$\n$h = 757999644$\n\nFinally, we XOR every vector with the translation $T = 904863744$. The hyperplane condition becomes $\\text{parity}(h \\ \\& \\ (y \\oplus T)) = 0$, meaning the updated offset is $c = \\text{parity}(h \\ \\& \\ T)$.\nThe bits of $T$ are `{13, 16, 17, 18, 19, 21, 22, 23, 24, 26, 28, 29}`.\nThe common set bits between $h$ and $T$ are `{13, 17, 18, 19, 21, 24, 26, 29}`.\nThere are exactly 8 common bits. The parity of 8 is 0, so $c = 0$.\n\n<answer>[757999644,0]</answer>", "error": null, "elapsed_sec": 94.26, "http_status": 200, "finish_reason": "stop", "preset": "hard", "escalation_round": 2}
