-{"schema_version": 2, "model": "google/gemini-3.1-pro-preview", "effort": "medium", "params": {"n": 280, "degree": 17, "removal_span": 64}, "seed": 36988770, "solved": "solved", "parsed": true, "verify_ok": true, "verify_reason": "ok", "reply": "To find the affine hyperplane containing every point of the final Sidon set M, we need to trace how the normal vector of the initial hyperplane transforms under the sequence of linear operations and the final translation.\n\n1. **Initial Hyperplane Normal**:\n   The initial retained points have `parity(h0 & v) = 0`, where `h0 = 131073`.\n   In binary, $h_0 = 2^{17} + 2^0$, meaning its 17th and 0th bits are 1, and all others are 0. So, our initial normal vector $h$ corresponds to the set of set bits $S = \\{0, 17\\}$. The initial offset is $c = 0$.\n\n2. **Linear Operations**:\n   The linear operations are applied to the points $v$. When an operation modifies the space of vectors $v$, we update the normal $h$ using the inverse-transpose map to keep the dot product $\\langle h, v \\rangle$ invariant:\n   - `swap i j`: Swaps coordinate $i$ and coordinate $j$ for the points. For the normal $h$, this corresponds to swapping $h_i$ and $h_j$.\n   - `xor s d`: Replaces $v_d$ with $v_d \\oplus v_s$. To keep $\\sum h_i v_i$ constant, the normal updates as $h_s \\leftarrow h_s \\oplus h_d$. (Notice that the dependency is reversed: $h_s$ changes based on the value of $h_d$).\n   \n   Starting with $S = \\{0, 17\\}$, we step systematically through all 280 operations, updating the bits of $h$ accordingly (toggling $h_s$ whenever $h_d = 1$, and swapping $h_i$ and $h_j$). \n   \n   After perfectly tracking all 280 operations, the 1-bits of the final normal vector $h$ end up at the following coordinates:\n   $S_{final} = \\{0, 1, 6, 7, 14, 15, 16, 17, 19, 21, 23, 24, 30, 31, 33\\}$\n\n   Calculating the integer value for the updated $h$:\n   $h = 2^0 + 2^1 + 2^6 + 2^7 + 2^{14} + 2^{15} + 2^{16} + 2^{17} + 2^{19} + 2^{21} + 2^{23} + 2^{24} + 2^{30} + 2^{31} + 2^{33}$\n   $h = 1 + 2 + 64 + 128 + 16384 + 32768 + 65536 + 131072 + 524288 + 2097152 + 8388608 + 16777216 + 1073741824 + 2147483648 + 8589934592$\n   $h = 11839193283$\n\n3. **Affine Translation**:\n   The final step XORs every vector with a translation $T = 17092114145$.\n   Translating the hyperplane offsets the constant $c$ by $\\langle h, T \\rangle \\pmod 2$. \n   The initial offset was $0$. Our newly offset parity equation becomes $c = \\text{parity}(h \\text{ \\& } T)$.\n   \n   Looking at the set bits of $T = 17092114145$, we have:\n   $T_{bits} = \\{0, 5, 6, 7, 9, 10, 12, 13, 14, 15, 18, 22, 23, 25, 27, 28, 29, 30, 31, 32, 33\\}$\n   \n   Intersecting $S_{final}$ with $T_{bits}$, the common bits are:\n   $\\{0, 6, 7, 14, 15, 23, 30, 31, 33\\}$\n   \n   There are exactly 9 elements in this intersection. Since 9 is odd, the parity bit of the dot product is $1$. So, the final offset is $c = 1$.\n\n<answer>[11839193283,1]</answer>", "error": null, "elapsed_sec": 122.0, "http_status": 200, "finish_reason": "stop", "preset": "escalated", "escalation_round": 3}
